{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790527959,"nonce":"Y8Zcm3iI3BsuELJ/MncrkDexT4I=","title":"Browser host: load a side module into a running program","message":"For hot reload in `caribou serve` (caribou git-bug\n47efa60d7bff0cbe4f71a72299616fd8ac3de96bf19616a9e800510bd8dfedfa): the dev\nserver compiles a changed Wren or Zyntax module into a wasm side module, and\nthe running program should load it without a page reload. Caribou tells the\nprogram which module changed; the reload itself (World::reload) is caribou's\nand WrenLift's.\n\nToday the browser host answers ash_host_dlopen/ash_host_dlsym with 0\n(browser/imports.rs), and side modules load only in the native host, before\nthe program starts (native/dylink.rs). Needed: the page host loads a side\nmodule into a program that is already running, against its memory and\nfunction table, and dlsym finds its exports.\n\nConstraint: the program's worker is blocked for as long as the program runs,\nso the load must be synchronous in that worker (sync XHR works in workers,\nthen WebAssembly.Module/Instance synchronously for small modules), or the\nmodule is handed over ahead of time -- the page posts the compiled module\nwhen the dev server announces it, and dlopen takes it from a queue.\n\nOverlaps aca00a1 (the browser loader, at start-up) and c639f0d (side modules\ncannot load into a threaded program: shared memory, and Rust's errno TLS\nrelocation) -- a threaded program is the one caribou serves.","files":null}]}